# The dependency list has two halves. The first is what hdw4s itself runs. The
# second, from libpulse0 down, is what upstream's Selkies package declares --
# duplicated deliberately, because hdw4s fetches that package and installs it
# with dpkg, and dpkg does not resolve dependencies: it unpacks over the
# working copy and only then refuses to configure. Declaring them here is what
# makes that install a decision rather than a gamble, and hdw4s-update checks
# the downloaded package's own list against the machine before it starts, so a
# dependency upstream adds is reported rather than discovered mid-unpack.
#
# What left the list when Selkies stopped being a wheel in a virtualenv hdw4s
# built: python3-venv (no virtualenv), python3-gi, python3-gst-1.0,
# gir1.2-gst* and gstreamer1.0-* (2.0 carries no GStreamer at all -- it encodes
# through its own pixelflux and pcmflux extension modules), python3-evdev
# (vendored inside the package), python3-setuptools (GPUtil's distutils import
# went with it) and xsel (the clipboard is in-process now).
# xdotool and ca-certificates are new: the first is the fallback Selkies 2.0
# forks for any keysym XTEST cannot inject, and the second is what lets curl
# reach GitHub at all, which the package has always needed and never said.
#
# python3-xlib left with the other vendored modules and came back as hdw4s's
# OWN dependency: hdw4s-stage-wait watches for the desktop to finish starting
# before the stream starts. Selkies' vendored copy is not used for that, because it
# is an upstream internal that moves when Selkies does. It is in universe, as
# xdotool already is, so this adds no archive component.
Package: hdw4s
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         hdw4s-shared-sweep (= ${binary:Version}),
         curl,
         ca-certificates,
         dbus-daemon,
         dbus-bin,
         gnome-session,
         nftables,
         iproute2,
         mawk | gawk,
         procps,
         systemd,
         libglib2.0-bin,
         dconf-cli,
         python3,
         python3-xlib,
         xauth,
         xdotool,
         x11-xserver-utils,
         xserver-xorg-core,
         xserver-xorg-video-dummy,
         libpulse0,
         libxcb1,
         libxkbcommon0,
         libx11-xcb1,
         libva2,
         libva-drm2,
         libva-x11-2,
         libdrm2,
         libgbm1,
         libegl1,
         libwayland-server0,
         libglib2.0-0t64 | libglib2.0-0,
         libpixman-1-0,
         libxcb-render0,
         libxcb-shm0,
         libxcb-dri3-0,
         libxfixes3,
         libxext6,
         libice6,
         libsm6
Recommends: pipewire-pulse, wireplumber, gnome-keyring, pulseaudio-utils
Suggests: msmtp
Description: headless GNOME desktop streamed to a web browser
 Gives an existing account on an existing machine a full GNOME desktop that is
 reachable from a browser, without containers and without a graphics card. A
 systemd template unit starts an X server on the dummy driver, a GNOME session
 and the Selkies streaming server, one instance per session.
 .
 Each session is allocated a port from a fixed block, so a reverse proxy in
 front of the machine can be configured once from a predictable mapping. The
 whole block is covered by the package's own nftables table, which restricts
 which addresses may reach it. A session starts when somebody first connects,
 rather than at boot, and is stopped again after a configurable idle period.
 .
 A session may optionally be isolated from any other desktop belonging to the
 same account, which allows a browser session to coexist with a desktop that is
 already logged in against the same shared home directory.

# The /shared tool on its own, for the machine that owns a store shared by
# several desktop machines -- a storage node or a hypervisor that runs no
# desktops and must not need hdw4s's dependencies to sweep it. hdw4s depends on
# it at the same version, so a desktop machine always has the tool its
# expose service and "hdw4s check" call. It carries hdw4s-duration because the
# tool parses durations and that grammar must stay one file.
Package: hdw4s-shared-sweep
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, python3
Description: guard, sweeper and usage relay for the hdw4s /shared drop area
 A /shared drop area is a filesystem every desktop can read and write, kept
 safe by its mount flags and kept tidy by this tool. It refuses to touch a
 store that is not a whole filesystem of its own mounted nosymfollow, nodev,
 noexec and nosuid, widens what is left there so everybody can use it,
 removes anything that is not a regular file or a directory, and expires
 what nobody has used for a configurable time.
 .
 Installed by hdw4s on every desktop machine. Install it on its own on the
 machine that owns a store shared over NFS or by bind mounts into containers,
 and run the sweeper there, where access times are truthful. An optional relay
 lets NFS clients report reads that never reach the server.
